@mtbrandy I'm pretty sure that's not the case - there are no TOC entries for function pointers, and all function calls correctly relocate under the `large` code model with the generated thunks. Also, there are far more than 3 different functions being called by `__gufunc__._ZN8__main__7foo$241Ei` through the thunk functions.

I'm certain that the problem is that all modules' TOC entries are relocated as if they were first.

But LLVM is an incredibly complex beast, and it has taken me days to isolate this particular problem from everything else, including the atomic refcounting and PPCCTRLoop noise. With more time I could perhaps find who is guilty of this failed relocation (Numba, llvmlite, LLVM?) as well. This sounds like a trivial but very damaging missed-offset or missed-list-of-GlobalVars problem.

@edelsohn Actually, now I do have an actual fix to offer to LLVM devs related to this issue.

Recall that I had mentioned earlier that part of the problem was the extreme displacement of the relocations, and that because of LLVM's choice of the `R_PPC64_REL32` within `.eh_frame` sections, that this would trigger an assert when LLVM is built in Debug mode.

@stuartarchibald has put up hours ago his wip/llvm6 branch on his repo and I happened to notice this only because I visited the Gitter numba/numba-dev channel and saw a reference to that branch.

However, I _also_ saw that @stuartarchibald mentioned aloud that an `llvm-elf-relocation.patch` was no longer necessary. Its name seemed _highly_ relevant, so I examined it. What I saw was that even though the cited patch is no longer necessary, only two lines above the following change relevant to us is required:

```diff
--- a/lib/MC/MCObjectFileInfo.cpp
+++ b/lib/MC/MCObjectFileInfo.cpp
@@ -289,6 +289,8 @@ void MCObjectFileInfo::initELFMCObjectFileInfo(Triple T) {
   case Triple::mips64el:
     FDECFIEncoding = dwarf::DW_EH_PE_sdata8;
     break;
+  case Triple::ppc64:
+  case Triple::ppc64le:
   case Triple::x86_64:
     FDECFIEncoding = dwarf::DW_EH_PE_pcrel |
                      (Large ? dwarf::DW_EH_PE_sdata8 : dwarf::DW_EH_PE_sdata4);
```

I then ran the Numba testsuite, and found that, along with the use of the `large` code model, this so far solved all of the `.eh_frame` troubles with `R_PPC64_REL32` even when I removed my ugly, temporary hack that prevented them from being emitted in the first place (forcing llvmlite to create all functions with `nounwind` attributes by modifying the line `llvmlite/ir/values.py:635` to `prototype = "{prefix} {name}({args}{vararg}) {attrs}{metadata}\n".format(` in `class Function`).

The segfaults due to the bad TOC relocations still happen with LLVM 6.0.0, and I'll keep digging, but I am satisfied that this patch is the solution to many of the other segfaults and would urge its inclusion in LLVM 6.0.1 and `trunk`. I admit that I do not 100% comprehend what changing `FDECFIEncoding` will do, but by breakpointing `llvm::RuntimeDyldELF::resolveRelocation` I can confirm that type-44 relocations (`R_PPC64_REL64`) rather than type-26 (`R_PPC64_REL32`) are now being emitted in `.eh_frame`, as was in fact my intention.

How and where may I submit this patch?

@edelsohn 

> Have you been able to develop any simplified testcase for the bad linkage and TOC relocation problem without fully debugging it? It seems to be an LLVM bug. It greatly would help to have some of the PPC64 LLVM developers assist with the triage if you can find a way to trigger it outside of numba.

I have not yet managed to do this, partly because to do this in pure C++ would require writing and wrestling with a fair amount of C++ boilerplate, and partly because it seems that an essential component of the bug is that Object 3 (and its TOC table) must be **JITed** into memory _strictly before_ Object 4 (and its own TOC table). An AOT compiler doesn't have that problem; It doesn't need to "make live and public" one Module's TOC table (and its layout) before adding the next, because it sees both Modules simultaneously and both of them before any of their code has been "made live and public", much less executed, anywhere.

@stuartarchibald I will look at `MemoryLeakMixin`, refcounting, possible deadlocks and bumping the timeout for linear algebra. I notice that `MemoryLeak` makes reads of several shared fields of `TheMSys` unprotected by `acquire` fences.

@stuartarchibald @edelsohn I also believe that [my reasoning](https://github.com/numba/numba/pull/2780#issue-172171471) in #2780 is bullet-proof, but it won't hurt for someone to double-check. In essence I argue that `monotonic` is sufficient, but for reference counter _decrements_ only, there must be

1) A `release` fence that orders all loads and stores before a successful atomic store of the new reference count, and
2) An `acquire` fence that only orders destructor loads and stores after the atomic load that feeds the successful atomic store.

For which I've made [PR 330](https://github.com/numba/llvmlite/pull/330) to llvmlite.

Lastly, I don't see `TheMSys.atomic_cas()` being used anywhere... What's it for?

