The symptom seems to be caused by the JIT believing that the call to foo in module2 with the linkonce_odr decoration can use the local entry point, which does not newly establish addressibility. module1 and module2 are JITted separately and apparently use separate TOC addressibility anchors.

The PPC64LE ELF backend keys off of SymType != SymbolRef::ST_Unknown to determine that the function call can use the local entrypoint.

Our hypothesis is that when rtdyld find the existing, strongly defined global symbol, and redirects to the existing symbol, it does not perform the necessary changes to the symbol flags / attributes to allow for the possibility that the call to the overriding function may be non-local and must be treated conservatively.

Based on the work-arounds so far, it looks like the logic in

```
    if (JITSymFlags.isWeak() || JITSymFlags.isCommon()) {
