@nemanjai There is a danger that by reversing 2. and 3. in `Module2`, you ordered the TOC tables of `Module2` in such a way as to make it compatible with `Module1`'s. I suspect that the codegen emits TOC entries in order of encounter, so if two functions require TOC entries, the first function compiled gets first spot in the TOC table.

If this happens, the code will use the wrong thing (an incorrect `r2` TOC pointer, due to entry by the local entry point) to fetch by pure coincidence the _right_ thing (the correct constant).

This only works if Module1 is strictly a prefix of Module2. In the case of Numba, they are often not.

@lhames @edelsohn @nemanjai From reading the ABI, the source code for `RuntimeDyld*.cpp` and more debugging (which, thanks to both my minimal reproduction code and `llvm-rtdyld`, is quite easy to do now outside of Numba) I have come to the view that this is specifically a **PPC64 ELFv2 ABI** `RuntimeDyldELF` bug and **not** a general bug with `RuntimeDyld`.

There is, to the best of my knowledge, no machine and no ABI other than PPC64 ELFv2 that specifies functions that simultaneously have all of

1) A globals pointer (GP) register.
3) A local entry point (LEP) that doesn't set the GP.
2) A distinct global entry point (GEP) that does set the GP.

The closest I'm aware of is

1) PPC64 ELFv1 and MIPS, whose functions have a GP (`r2` for PPC64; `gp` for MIPS) and a GEP, but no LEP because function pointers and interposable function calls read a pointer to the GEP from a function descriptor (in `.opd`). The function always enters by the GEP and unconditionally sets the GP.
2) Similarly, Itanium/IA-64, whose functions have a GP (`gp`) and a LEP, but no GEP because function pointers and interposable function calls read a pointer to the LEP and a new value of the GP from a function descriptor. The function always enters by the LEP and the caller has already set GP properly.

Apparently, only PPC64 ELFv2 has:

1) A GEP and LEP of the same nature (both are locations in `.text`, and neither is a mere descriptor),
2) The possibility of distinct values for GEP and LEP, such that the GEP falls through to the LEP, and
3) A function pointer that can meaningfully point either to the LEP or GEP with only a relocation.

Since the only format PPC64 appears to support is ELF (Mach-O and COFF are not supported), and PPC64's LEP and GEP handling is so unique, the only place where code for relaxing from GEP to LEP exists is in `RuntimeDyldELF.cpp`. The problem is not truly in `RuntimeDyld.cpp`: `RuntimeDyldImpl::loadObjectImpl()` handles **symbols**, but the problem we are facing is at the level of **relocations** to symbols.

The hack that @edelsohn proposed happened to fix things, but altered the global symbol table in unacceptable ways in pursuit of the true goal, which is preventing relaxation of a call to GEP into a call to LEP. Due to the way `RuntimeDyldELF.cpp` is coded right now, changing the type of a function symbol to `ST_Unknown` achieves the correct aim, but we are fundamentally lying through our teeth when we know it's a `ST_Function`, and are masking the bug, not fixing it.

# Proposed Patch

This is why I propose the following patch, which I've tested and which solves our segfault problems as well. This patch only affects `R_PPC64_REL24` relocations, which are only ever used by PPC64 branch instructions and never by anyone else. It also only affects the ELFv2 ABI.

Given that we do not know

- Which function, if any, a `R_PPC64_REL24` relocation is in,
- Whether that `R_PPC64_REL24` relocation points to a function or function stub and
- Whether that function's `r2` matches this function's `r2`,

I used the **heuristic** `Value.SectionID != SectionID` to guess at whether a branch is an inter-module call. **This heuristic works well, and `make check-llvm` reports no failures.** It is not a perfect heuristic, but different Objects' sections are assigned different IDs, and that is why this code is _guaranteed_ to catch the case of inter-Object calls that is currently breaking. It will _not_ detect an intra-Object call between functions with different TOC pointers, but this case is already being handled properly by the static linker.

