OK, so I dove into this to see what is going on. Turns out that loop rotation is duplicating the PPC intrinsics. The below patch will prevent it from doing that and should fix your problem.
However, I'm a little concerned about the pipeline you described above. You are running codegen passes on the module and then passing that back through the middle end. I don't really understand the reason for that. It certainly has caused failures in this case and is generally not something that is well tested (certainly not on PPC). I don't imagine that there's anything that explicitly makes this invalid, but I guess I just don't really understand the reasoning behind it.
In any case, given this pipeline, we should think of ways of improving our testing on PPC to replicate this pipeline and test with that.

Patch:
```
Index: include/llvm/IR/IntrinsicsPowerPC.td
===================================================================
--- include/llvm/IR/IntrinsicsPowerPC.td        (revision 329903)
+++ include/llvm/IR/IntrinsicsPowerPC.td        (working copy)
@@ -36,8 +36,9 @@
 
   // Intrinsics used to generate ctr-based loops. These should only be
   // generated by the PowerPC backend!
-  def int_ppc_mtctr : Intrinsic<[], [llvm_anyint_ty], []>;
-  def int_ppc_is_decremented_ctr_nonzero : Intrinsic<[llvm_i1_ty], [], []>;
+  def int_ppc_mtctr : Intrinsic<[], [llvm_anyint_ty], [IntrNoDuplicate]>;
+  def int_ppc_is_decremented_ctr_nonzero : Intrinsic<[llvm_i1_ty], [],
+                                                     [IntrNoDuplicate]>;
 
   // Intrinsics for [double]word extended forms of divide instructions
   def int_ppc_divwe : GCCBuiltin<"__builtin_divwe">,
```

@nemanjai I understand your concern and this is something that aroused my suspicions as well. However, currently that's what Numba does, and it might require wider efforts from the developers to change that.

I was mildly surprised to see `ppc` CTR intrinsics in bona-fide LLVM IR; I thought this was an optimization introduced at machine-instruction level, and so would never be seen in IR. But I am also mildly surprised that re-optimizing CTR intrinsics broke them, although doing this does sound like asking for trouble. Presumably generic optimization passes like LICM and loop-rotate are not taught all of the particularities of every target-specific intrinsic; This would be too much.

I'll test your proposed patch promptly.

